{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"d23e ROUND 4 = BLOCK, 8 findings, all remediated at the root; round 5 launched. The 8 collapsed into 3 real defects:\n\n(a) FIVE of the eight were ONE point restated: my AC said the deprecation warning fires \"once per process\", and there is NO MECHANISM for that. VERIFIED at _deprecations.py:299-329 — warn_deprecated emits UNCONDITIONALLY: no _seen set, no _emitted flag, no warnings filter, and none of the six existing env deprecations dedupes either. I INVENTED the requirement. WITHDRAWN rather than implemented: adding per-process state for one surface would make it the odd one out for no stated benefit.\n\n(b) THE REAL ERROR, and a repeat of a pattern worth naming: I resolved a design question against the WRONG AUTHORITY. Round 3 I switched the deprecation to `_permanent(...)` because all six existing env entries use it. But the PARENT eb58 says in bold that REBAR_LLM_MODEL is \"DEPRECATED with a migration window rather than kept as a permanent alias\", with a rationale. The parent CONSIDERED and REJECTED the permanent alias. I checked the repo's precedents and never opened the parent's plan. Reverted to `_scheduled(...)`, remove_in=_MAJOR. LESSON: repo convention loses to a parent's explicit, reasoned decision — and 'check what the repo actually does' must be paired with 'check what the parent already decided'.\n\n(c) The four example configs were in Scope with NO acceptance criterion, so they could have shipped as prose ABOUT configs. Added an AC requiring four complete pasteable tables.\n\nTWO PROCESS FAILURES FROM THIS ROUND, both worth carrying:\n* My fix for (b) left the plan SELF-CONTRADICTORY in two places (Approach still promised the withdrawn once-per-process; the S8 safety passage still called `_permanent` \"the form this plan adopts\", and that passage's whole safety argument rested on remove_in=None). Re-reading the WHOLE plan after editing caught both — the discipline works, but only if actually run.\n* MY VERIFICATION OF ONE FIX GAVE A FALSE GREEN. I checked for the stale phrase with a literal string that had a LINE BREAK inside it in the stored text — so the regex silently failed to match AND my count() check returned 0, agreeing with each other while the stale text sat there. Checking a fix with the same broken assumption that caused the miss proves nothing. Re-checked whitespace-tolerantly and the passage was still stale.\n\nKnock-on: the S8 kill-switch DURABILITY concern on eb6e (id eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a) is REINSTATED — my withdrawal of it depended on _permanent. Second flip; recorded as a sequence on the ticket. Not a safety issue (in-window the var warns and works); it is that an S8 runbook naming the var rots at v1.0.0. Still the operator's call, still not edited into eb6e's plan."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785587069952995001,"uuid":"e91dbd09-e3ff-435e-928c-7e45baa3d6d9"}